Short answer: No — just changing “Custom Base URL” to https://XXXX:8081 will NOT magically enable HTTPS.
You must actually configure HTTPS (SSL/TLS) on your JFrog Artifactory or on the reverse proxy (Nginx/Apache/Traefik/HAProxy).

Let me explain clearly.

✅ Why “Custom Base URL = https://XXXX:8081” does NOT enable HTTPS
Custom Base URL only affects:

the URLs JFrog shows in UI

links sent in emails

repository links displayed during integration

It does NOT create an HTTPS listener.

If your server is listening only on:

http://XXXX:8081
then even if you set:

Custom Base URL = https://XXXX:8081
JFrog will still serve only HTTP, so Maven will fail with SSL errors.

✅ The correct method to enable HTTPS
You have two valid ways:

Option 1: Enable HTTPS using a Reverse Proxy (recommended for Maven)
Use Nginx/Apache:

1. Install Nginx (example)
sudo apt install nginx
2. Configure Nginx SSL
Create a config:

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name your-domain.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/artifact.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/artifact.key;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8081/;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
    }
}
3. Restart Nginx
sudo systemctl restart nginx
Now your JFrog will be available at:

https://your-domain.com
4. Now update Custom Base URL to:
https://your-domain.com
✔ This is correct and works with Maven.

Option 2: Enable HTTPS directly in Artifactory (not preferred)
Artifactory can be configured to listen on HTTPS via tomcat/server.xml (if older version) or router TLS (if newer JFrog version).
But this is more complex and not recommended.

Most production setups use reverse proxy.

🔧 What Maven needs
In settings.xml, Maven expects:

<url>https://your-domain.com/artifactory/... </url>
This will only work if your server really supports HTTPS, not only configured in UI.

🔥 Final Answer
Custom Base URL = https://XXXX:8081 alone will NOT enable HTTPS and Maven will not work.

You must configure HTTPS using a reverse proxy or configure TLS inside Artifactory.

Once HTTPS is real and working → then update Custom Base URL.

